      "files": ["src/policy/outbound.js", "src/routes/preview.js", "src/http/client.js"],
      "evidence": "preview.js reads req.query.url, validates it with outbound.isAllowed(url), then calls get(url, {followRedirects:true}) which does fetch(url,{redirect:'follow'}) and returns the response body to the client. isAllowed() is implemented as `url.startsWith('https://trusted.example.test')`. String.startsWith is a prefix check, not an origin check, so it is bypassable: (a) userinfo form `https://trusted.example.test@evil.com/` startsWith-matches yet resolves to host evil.com; (b) subdomain-suffix `https://trusted.example.test.evil.com/` also matches and resolves to a fully attacker-controlled host. Verified at runtime: both inputs return isAllowed()===true while new URL(...).hostname is evil.com / trusted.example.test.evil.com. Because the attacker controls the resolved host, redirect:'follow' is applied, and the body is returned, the server can be made to fetch arbitrary external or internal addresses (e.g. localhost, RFC1918, cloud metadata) and leak their contents. The sibling avatar.js route is NOT affected because it uses policy/origin.js exactTrustedOrigin(), which parses the URL and checks protocol==='https:' && hostname==='images.example.test' && port==='' (verified to reject both bypasses) and uses redirect:'manual'.",
      "remediation": "Replace the prefix check with strict URL parsing: parse with new URL(url), require protocol==='https:', compare parsed.hostname for exact equality to the trusted host (reject userinfo, subdomain suffixes, and trailing dots), and require parsed.port to be the expected/default port. Do not rely on startsWith. Additionally, in http/client.js either disable redirect following for outbound fetches or re-run the same strict origin validation against each redirect Location before following it, since redirect:'follow' can otherwise traverse the trust boundary to internal hosts."
    },
    {
      "category": "open-redirect",
      "severity": "MEDIUM",
      "files": ["src/routes/continue.js"],
      "evidence": "The /continue handler does `res.redirect(String(req.query.returnTo || '/'))` with no validation of scheme or host. An attacker can set ?returnTo=https://evil.com/phish (or //evil.com) and the server responds with a 302 whose Location is that attacker-controlled absolute URL. Because the redirect is issued by the trusted origin, it is useful for phishing and for stealing tokens/credentials in redirect-based flows (e.g. OAuth continuation).",
      "remediation": "Only allow same-origin relative targets: verify returnTo starts with a single '/' and does not start with '//', reject any value parseable as an absolute or protocol-relative URL, and optionally restrict to a known path whitelist. Never pass user-controlled absolute URLs straight to res.redirect."
    }
  ],
